// rbac domain -- the consolidated, DSL-defined authorization model.
//
// Epic 1 (memql#2062) replaces the scattered, Go-hardcoded role
// constants in component/auth/rbac.go with a SINGLE DSL-defined RBAC
// model: primitive verbs composed into ranked roles, governed by
// relational predicates, and extensible with data-defined custom
// roles. This file lands E1.1 (memql#2069): the primitive verbs and
// the capability model.
//
// THE MODEL, in one paragraph. Authorization is expressed as
// CAPABILITIES. A capability is a single (verb x resourceType) grant
// attached to a role. The five verbs are uniform across EVERY resource
// type -- including the `principal` resource (a user/identity), which
// is how user-management governance (E1.3, #2071) is expressed without
// a bespoke grant verb: "manage a developer" is just `update` on a
// principal of rank developer, gated by a rank+self predicate. Roles
// (E1.2, #2070) are named, ranked bundles of capabilities. A role's
// effective grant set is the union of its capability rows.
//
// Per-row authz tier: PUBLIC reference data. The capability catalog is
// global, immutable, deployment-wide configuration (authored as `seed`
// rows, materialized into _system). It carries no per-user ownership
// and is readable by every authenticated principal so any node can
// resolve "does role R hold capability (verb,resourceType)?" locally
// without a per-row owner check. Writes are seed-only (E1.2 marks base
// roles immutable at runtime; custom-role capabilities in E1.4 are
// minted through a rank-bounded mutation, not free-form writes).

/// A single authorization GRANT: the right to apply one primitive verb to one resource type,
/// attached to a role. Capability = (verb x resourceType) for a role. This is the atomic unit of
/// the consolidated RBAC model (epic memql#2062). The five verbs
/// (read/create/update/delete/execute) are UNIFORM across every resourceType, including `principal`
/// -- applying a verb to the principal resource is how user-management governance is expressed
/// (E1.3): e.g. `update` on a principal, gated by a rank+self predicate, means 'edit a user';
/// `create` on a principal means 'add a user' WITHOUT necessarily being able to edit existing ones
/// (the create != update split). A role's effective grants are the union of its capability rows.
/// Global / immutable reference data: authored as `seed` rows materialized into _system, readable
/// by every principal, never per-user-owned. PUBLIC per-row-authz tier -- it is deployment-wide
/// config, not user data. roleSlug points at the role this grant belongs to; the role rows
/// themselves land in E1.2 (memql#2070), so until then capabilities reference a role purely by its
/// stable slug string.
@displayCard(primary="resourceType", secondary="verb", tertiary="roleSlug", status="effect")
concept capability {
  roleSlug      string!  @description("Stable kebab-case slug of the role this capability is granted to (e.g. 'owner', 'developer', 'admin', 'user', or a custom-role slug). A foreign-key-by-slug into the v1:rbac:role catalog -- matched against role.slug, not the role's node id (mirrors how v1:agents:agent.roleSlug references v1:agents:agentRole.slug). A role's effective capability set is every capability row with a matching roleSlug.")
  verb          enum("read", "create", "update", "delete", "execute")!  @description("The primitive verb this capability grants. The five verbs are the COMPLETE, uniform set across every resourceType -- there is deliberately no separate `grant`/`manage`/`admin` verb. read = observe a resource. create = bring a new resource into existence. update = modify an existing resource. delete = remove a resource. execute = run/invoke a resource (a tool, an automation, a deploy action). The create != update split is load-bearing for governance (E1.3): a role can hold `create` on principals of a given rank without holding `update`, i.e. 'mint developers but not edit existing ones'.")
  resourceType  string!  @description("The kind of resource the verb applies to. Uniform verb set means the SAME five verbs are meaningful against every resourceType. Canonical values seeded in this domain: `principal` (a user/identity -- the governance-bearing resource; verbs on it gate user management per E1.3), `construct` (DSL authoring -- create/update/delete map to the MCP define/author surface, execute maps to inline DSL), `data` (graph rows -- the read/write data plane), `deployment` (cut-version / deploy / rollback actions, reached via `execute`), `agent` (agent templates), `group` (org groups), `role` (the RBAC role catalog itself -- create/update/delete on `role` is what bounds custom-role authoring in E1.4). resourceType is an open string (not a closed enum) so product layers can introduce their own resource kinds without an engine change; the seeded set above is the core vocabulary.")
  effect        enum("allow", "deny")!  @default("allow")  @description("Whether this capability GRANTS (allow) or explicitly REVOKES (deny) the (verb,resourceType) for the role. allow is the overwhelming common case and the default. deny exists so a higher-ranked role or a custom role can carve a hole in an otherwise-broad grant (e.g. a finance role that is otherwise admin-like but must never `execute` on `deployment`); a deny always wins over an allow at resolution time. E1.6 (memql#2074) resolves an effective decision as: an allow capability exists AND no deny capability exists for the same (role,verb,resourceType).")
  description   string  @description("Human-readable note explaining WHY this grant exists. Surfaced in the cockpit RBAC view. Optional; seeded capabilities carry one for auditability.")
  predefined    bool    @default("false")  @description("True for capability rows authored by dsl/rbac/*.memql `seed` declarations on every startup -- the immutable base-role grant set (E1.2 marks these read-only at runtime so a base role can never be redefined to escalate). User-/custom-role capabilities minted via the rank-bounded mutation (E1.4) default to predefined=false and are editable within their creator's rank bound.")
  active        bool    @default("true")  @description("Soft-deactivate flag. An inactive capability is ignored at resolution time but kept for audit. Lets a custom role's grant be retired without churning the row history.")
}

/// A named, RANKED bundle of capabilities -- the role half of the consolidated RBAC model (epic
/// memql#2062). A role's effective authorization is the union of its v1:rbac:capability grants
/// (joined by roleSlug). E1.2 (memql#2070) authors the FOUR base roles as immutable seed rows:
/// owner (400) > developer (300) > admin (200) > user (100). The rank ordering is the locked
/// decision -- developer OUTRANKS admin (the power axes differ: developer is engineering authority,
/// admin is user-management authority) and owner is self-managed only. Ranks are spaced integers
/// (not 1/2/3/4) precisely so E1.4 (memql#2072) custom roles can SLOT BETWEEN existing ranks (e.g.
/// a 'lead' at 250 sits above admin but below developer) without renumbering. The governance
/// predicates in E1.3 (memql#2071) compare actor.rank vs target.rank to decide who-can-manage-whom.
/// Base roles are IMMUTABLE at runtime: predefined=true rows are re-materialized from this DSL on
/// every boot and a Go pre-insert guard (validateRbacBaseRoleImmutable) rejects any
/// non-system-actor write to them, so a base role can never be redefined as data to escalate
/// privilege. Custom roles (predefined=false) are addable as DATA via the rank-bounded createRole
/// path in E1.4. Global / _system reference data: readable by every principal so any node resolves
/// a rank locally; PUBLIC per-row-authz tier.
@displayCard(primary="name", secondary="slug", tertiary="description", status="active")
concept role {
  slug         string!  @description("Stable kebab-case identifier -- the value v1:rbac:capability.roleSlug points to and the value a principal's role resolves to. Never renamed (rename = new row + migration). The four base slugs are 'owner', 'developer', 'admin', 'user'; custom roles pick their own stable slug.")
  name         string!  @description("Display name shown in the cockpit RBAC view and role pickers, e.g. 'Owner', 'Developer', 'Admin', 'Member'.")
  rank         int!  @description("Numeric privilege rank -- HIGHER is more privileged (the inverse of the legacy auth.RoleLevel where lower=higher; the DSL model flips it so 'higher rank' reads naturally). The locked base ranks are owner=400 > developer=300 > admin=200 > user=100. Spaced by 100 so custom roles slot between without renumbering (E1.4). The E1.3 governance predicates gate management on actor.rank > target.rank (plus the self / owner-only carve-outs). A creator minting a custom role in E1.4 may not set a rank at or above their own -- enforced by the validateRbacCustomRoleRankBound Go guard (a creator can only author roles STRICTLY BELOW their own rank).")
  description  string  @description("One- or two-line description of the role's authority. Surfaced in the role picker's expanded view and the cockpit RBAC audit surface.")
  predefined   bool    @default("false")  @description("True for the four base-role rows authored by dsl/rbac/seeds.memql on every startup. predefined=true marks the row IMMUTABLE at runtime: the validateRbacBaseRoleImmutable Go guard rejects any non-system-actor write to it (a base role cannot be redefined / re-ranked / deactivated as data -- the escalation-by-redefinition block). User-created custom roles (E1.4) default to predefined=false and are editable within their creator's rank bound.")
  active       bool    @default("true")  @description("Soft-deactivate flag. An inactive role disappears from pickers but principals already carrying it keep resolving (their grants still apply) until reassigned. Base roles are never deactivated (the immutability guard blocks it).")
}
